11
Offline-first store-and-forward sync
Buses often lose network in tunnels, low-coverage areas, etc. Instead of dropping data, the edge device buffers detections locally and auto-syncs the moment connectivity returns — so no event is lost even in dead zones
Local SQLite/embedded DB on the edge device as a buffer queue; background sync service (Python + requests retry logic, or MQTT with persistent session/QoS1) that flushes queued data on reconnect; delta-sync (only send unsent records, avoid duplicates via unique event IDs
12
Fleet-consensus validation
If multiple buses independently flag the same pothole/defect at the same GPS point within a time window, auto-escalate confidence/priority — reduces false positives, turns noisy detections into reliable, evidence-backed alerts
Geo-clustering logic (DBSCAN on lat/long + timestamp window) on the backend to group repeated detections into one verified event with a rising confidence score
14
Severity + cost-to-fix scoring
Instead of just plotting dots on a map, auto-rank each detected issue by severity/urgency so the municipal dashboard becomes a prioritized action queue, not a flat list
Simple scoring model combining: defect size (bounding box area as proxy), frequency of repeat detection (from Module 12), and road category (arterial vs. minor road, from GIS metadata) → weighted score → sorted work-order list




Privacy & compliance angle (judges specifically probe this)
Bus cameras capture faces, license plates of innocent vehicles, and inside-cabin footage of passengers/children. You need a story for this or a judge will ask and expose a gap.
Auto-blur/anonymize faces and non-flagged plates before any data leaves the edge device (only unblur if legally flagged as an incident, with audit trail)
Mention data retention policy (e.g., raw footage auto-deleted after 7 days unless linked to an active incident)
This single addition signals you've thought about real-world deployment, not just a hackathon toy.
